wasi-sh: let a shell run inside a shell.

Applied AFTER ash-shebang.patch, which is what makes it matter: `#!/bin/sh`
is the most common shebang there is, and it lands on the `sh` applet, which
is this shell again.

`sh script`, `sh -c …` and `ash …` have always been reachable by typing
them, and have always been destructive. Upstream ash is a process-per-shell
program; run_nofork_applet() calls ash_main() again inside the same
instance, and two things went wrong there:

  1. exitshell() ends in _exit(), which is proc_exit in a wasm build. It
     ended the WHOLE INSTANCE, so `echo a; sh -c 'echo n'; echo b` printed
     a and n and then stopped, with status 0 and no error, and an
     interactive session just ended at the prompt. It now goes out through
     die_func when one is installed, which is precisely when this shell is
     a nested one, so the nested shell becomes a command that returned a
     status.

  2. ash_main() runs every INIT_G() unconditionally: three globals structs,
     the command hash table, the alias table. On top of that this file
     carries mutable statics. The outer shell's whole state is saved around
     the call and put back — the variable scope it was in, its command and
     alias tables, its loop and function nesting, the parser's pushback and
     here-documents, the expander's scratch.

The state is written once as an X-macro so the save and the restore cannot
drift apart, and build/build.sh re-derives the same list from the patched
source: a busybox update that adds a mutable static fails the build instead
of quietly corrupting one more thing.

Nesting is capped at 8. There is no process to fall over and no fork to
fail, so a script that runs itself would recurse until the wasm stack traps
— which is unrecoverable and takes the session, the filesystem and every
warm instance with it.

Not undone: the nested shell's own allocations, a few KB abandoned per
nested shell. That is the bargain every applet in this build makes.

